              "text": "The best and most efficient way to change the color of a `<span>` on hover is by using **CSS**. However, you can also achieve this using **JavaScript** or **inline HTML attributes**. \n\nHere are the three ways to do it, ordered from best practice to alternative methods:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: Using CSS (Recommended)\nThis is the cleanest and most standard method. It separates your presentation (CSS) from your structure (HTML) and allows you to add a smooth transition animation easily.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <style>\n    /* 1. Define the initial color */\n    .color-change-span {\n      color: black;\n      font-size: 18px;\n      cursor: pointer; /* Optional: Changes cursor to a hand */\n      transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: Makes the color change smooth */\n    }\n\n    /* 2. Define the hover color */\n    .color-change-span:hover {\n      color: #ff4757; /* Changes to a coral/red color on hover */\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n  <span class=\"color-change-span\">Hover over me to change my color!</span>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: Using Inline HTML (Quickest/No external CSS)\nIf you are working in a environment where you cannot edit a separate stylesheet, you can use HTML\u2019s built-in `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes.\n\n```html\n<span \n  style=\"color: black; transition: color 0.3s;\" \n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='blue'\" \n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black'\">\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n*Note: While fast, this is generally discouraged for larger projects because it mixes logic/styling with your HTML.*\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: Using JavaScript (For dynamic logic)\nIf you need to change the color based on user interactions, database values, or complex logic, you can use JavaScript event listeners.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<body>\n\n  <span id=\"my-span\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n  <script>\n    const span = document.getElementById('my-span');\n\n    // Change color when mouse enters\n    span.addEventListener('mouseover', () => {\n      span.style.color = 'green';\n    });\n\n    // Reset color when mouse leaves\n    span.addEventListener('mouseout', () => {\n      span.style.color = 'black';\n    });\n  </script>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Which one should you choose?\n* Use **Method 1 (CSS)** for 99% of use cases. It is the most performant and the easiest to maintain.\n* Use **Method 2 (Inline)** if you are writing quick emails, working in a restricted CMS (like Shopify or WordPress text editors), or testing something rapidly.\n* Use **Method 3 (JavaScript)** if you want other complex actions to trigger at the exact same time the color changes.",
